          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-tenant-of-wildfell-hall-86bfe2a584b3fb03\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Huntingdon explicitly presents his love for Helen as information about his emotional state, making T dominant. The declaration occurs within a marriage proposal and thus has weak undertaking-related force, but the words themselves chiefly avow love rather than constitute a commitment. Despite the hyperbole and excitement, nothing indicates that the words escape reflexively or function as affective discharge independently of the avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The proposal context gives slight P support, although the explicit undertaking is principally carried by the subsequent request that Helen bestow herself upon him.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Huntingdon directly addresses Helen in dialogue represented within her first-person narrative: “I love you to distraction!”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Huntingdon frames the statement as consequential “intelligence,” contrasts Helen with Annabella, and immediately presses Helen for a favorable response and marriage commitment. His hyperbole, presumptuous interpretation of silence, physical pressure, and threats of madness make the courtship coercive and theatrically intense, but these contextual features do not alter the core utterance’s dominant force as an avowal.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“and I love you to distraction!—Now, tell me if that intelligence gives you any pleasure.”\",\"supports\":\"The description of the declaration as “intelligence” strongly supports truth-conditional presentation of a loving state; “to distraction” supplies intensity but not independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"E2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Then let me add, that I cannot live without you ... Will you bestow yourself upon me?”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration forms part of a marriage proposal, providing limited performative or undertaking-related support while locating the clearest commitment-seeking force in the subsequent question.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"E3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Huntingdon resumes a prepared comparison between Annabella and Helen, demands an answer, construes Helen’s silence as assent, and embraces her forcefully.\",\"supports\":\"The structured, strategically pressing delivery weighs against interpreting the love words as a reflex-like verbal discharge; it also supplies the coercive courtship context.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"E4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit “I love you” in Anne Brontë’s The Tenant of Wildfell Hall.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the targeted core expression and its present-state avowal form.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core force: a strong avowal with weak proposal-associated undertaking force and no independently evidenced expressive/reflexive discharge. The coercion and theatrical hyperbole belong to contextual interpretation rather than a missing core category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
